Client
  ↓
[Controller]  ← receives HTTP request, validates DTOs
  ↓
[Service]     ← business logic, calls repo
  ↓
[Repository]  ← database access with JPA
  ↓
[Entity]      ← database mapping

(Parallel to this: security filter intercepts request first)

[Security Filter] → validates JWT → loads user details → sets SecurityContext

Exception handler intercepts errors globally

Utility classes used anywhere as helpers


1️)Client Sends HTTP Request → controller Layer

The client (browser/app/Postman) sends a REST API request (e.g., login, get user info).

The controller class in com.jsp.usermanagement.controller receives the request.

Controller validates request body (using DTOs from dto package).

Controller calls the appropriate service method.

Interview highlight:

Controller handles incoming requests, validates data, and delegates to service layer.

2️) Controller Calls Business Logic → service Layer

com.jsp.usermanagement.service contains interfaces and/or classes implementing core business logic.

Service methods handle things like authentication, user creation, updating data.

Service layer uses DTOs to structure input/output and may perform additional validation or processing.

Interview highlight:

Service layer contains business logic and coordinates between controller and repository.

3️) Service Interacts With Database → repository Layer

Service calls repository interfaces inside com.jsp.usermanagement.repository.

These interfaces extend JpaRepository or similar and automatically provide CRUD operations.

Repositories query or update the database entities.

Interview highlight:

Repository abstracts database operations using JPA, providing a clean interface.

4️) Database Mapping → entity Layer

Repository deals with entity classes inside com.jsp.usermanagement.entity.

Entities are JPA-annotated classes mapping Java objects to database tables.

Entity fields correspond to table columns.

Interview highlight:

Entity classes map database tables to Java objects, enabling ORM.

5️) Data Transfer Objects → dto Layer

DTO classes (com.jsp.usermanagement.dto) represent data structures for requests and responses.

Used to transfer data safely between client and server without exposing internal entities.

Example: LoginRequest, LoginResponse, UserResponse.

Interview highlight:

DTOs ensure clean API contracts and prevent exposing sensitive internal data.

6️) Security Layer In Action → security Package

This is a critical part of your JWT app.

Before controller receives request, JWT filters (e.g., JwtAuthenticationFilter) intercept HTTP requests.

Filter extracts the JWT token from headers.

Calls JWT utility (JwtUtil or similar) to validate token signature and expiry.

Loads user details via CustomUserDetailsService (which fetches user from DB).

Creates Authentication object and sets it in Spring Security’s SecurityContext.

SecurityConfig sets up the entire chain: disabling sessions, CSRF, adding JWT filter.

Interview highlight:

Security layer authenticates requests statelessly using JWT, integrating with Spring Security filters.

7️) Exception Handling → exception Package

If any error happens (e.g., invalid token, bad request), custom exceptions can be thrown.

Centralized @ControllerAdvice handles these exceptions and returns consistent error responses.

Interview highlight:

Exception layer provides centralized error handling and clean API error messages.

8️) Utility Classes → utility Package

Contains helper functions used across layers (e.g., date formatting, string helpers).

Keeps code DRY (Don’t Repeat Yourself).

Interview highlight:

Utility classes encapsulate reusable logic to keep code clean.

9️) Optional: config Package (If Present)

Beans like PasswordEncoder (BCrypt) and CORS config live here.

Helps keep config modular and maintainable.


***********************************************************************
 The app follows layered architecture. When a client sends a request, the controller receives and validates it using DTOs.
The controller calls the service layer, which contains the business logic. Service interacts with the repository layer 
to perform database operations on entities.
Before the controller processes the request, the JWT security filter intercepts it to extract and validate the token,
sets the user authentication context, allowing stateless authentication.
Exception handling is centralized to provide consistent error responses. Utilities provide reusable helpers. 
This separation ensures clean, maintainable code.

