1️) REST API — The Communication Bridge
REST API stands for Representational State Transfer Application Programming Interface.
It is a way for clients (like web browsers, mobile apps, Postman) to communicate with your backend server.
You design REST endpoints (URLs) to send and receive data in JSON format over HTTP.

Example endpoints:
POST /login → User login
GET /users → Get list of users
Why REST API?
Language-agnostic (any client can use it)
Stateless (each request is independent)
Standard HTTP methods (GET, POST, PUT, DELETE)

2️) Java Spring Boot Project (Eclipse IDE) — The Backend Server
Your backend application is written in Java using Spring Boot framework.
You write Java classes to:
Define REST controllers (handle HTTP requests)
Implement business logic (services)
Map data to database (entities & repositories)
Configure security (Spring Security + JWT)
You use Eclipse IDE to write, compile, and run your Java Spring Boot project locally.
When you run the app, it starts an embedded server (usually Tomcat) on your machine, 
e.g. at http://localhost:8080.

3️) Postman — The API Testing Tool (Client)
Postman is a desktop/web application used to test your REST APIs.
It acts as a client that sends HTTP requests to your Spring Boot server.
You use Postman to:
Send POST /login with username and password JSON body
Receive JWT token in response
Send protected requests with Authorization: Bearer <token> header
Inspect responses and status codes
Postman mimics what frontend or mobile apps do but in a simple, controlled way.

4️)Database (PostgreSQL) — The Data Store
PostgreSQL is your relational database.
It stores your app’s persistent data — users, roles, tokens, etc.
Your Spring Boot project connects to PostgreSQL using JDBC and JPA.
When your app runs queries (via repositories), data is fetched or saved in PostgreSQL.
Your application.properties configures connection details (url, username, password).

*** How They Work Together — Step-by-Step Flow
Step	What Happens
1	You write backend code in Eclipse and run Spring Boot app locally. Server listens on port 8080.
2	You open Postman and send a HTTP request (e.g., login).
3	Postman sends JSON request over network to your Spring Boot REST API endpoint (e.g., /login).
4	Spring Boot controller receives request, calls service layer, checks database via repositories.
5	Database (PostgreSQL) returns data or updates it based on the query.
6	Your app creates a JWT token after successful login and sends JSON response back.
7	Postman shows you the response (token). You use token in future requests.
8	When you send other requests, Spring Security checks JWT token validity before allowing access.

#### Why This Matters for Interview
Interviewers want to see you understand:
Roles of each component (Eclipse, Spring Boot, Postman, PostgreSQL)
How client-server communication works over REST
How JWT fits in authentication flow
How database integrates with backend

